Good instinct — and the answer is the nicest rule in Lua: **looking never uses anything.** Only that `inv[item] = inv[item] - 1` line inside useItem consumes. Printing just reads.

So to check your water without touching it:

```lua
print(inventory.water)
```

Prints `2`, and you still have 2. Want it labeled:

```lua
print("Water: " .. inventory.water)
```

Prints `Water: 2`. For a multi-word name, read it back with the same brackets you wrote it with:

```lua
print(inventory["grape blunt"])
```

One warning about the line you wrote — `print(useItem(inventory, "water"))`: wrapping useItem in print doesn't stop it using the item, because useItem does its subtracting *inside itself* before print ever sees anything. And since your useItem returns nothing, that line would also print a stray `nil` under its message.

Rule of thumb: **print reads, functions act.** If you only want to look, print the table value directly and never call the function at all.